<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islam III Giray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1654</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> III Giray was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> from 1644 to 1654. In 1648 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> uprising against Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> forces helped win the battles of Zhovti Vody and Korsun, but he repeatedly made his own terms with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and withdrew at Berestechko in 1651, contributing to the Cossack defeat. The alliance brought the Tatars captives and influence across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p></div>
